'''Haramara''', officially the '''Socialist Republic of Haramara''', is an island nation in the Capuyaquiran Sea comprising a principal island and roughly 50 smaller islands. It has a population of approximately 8.1 million and an economy heavily dependent on oil exports. Haramara describes its political system as democratic syndicalism and organizes much of its economy through worker cooperatives and trade unions.

==Etymology==
The islands were originally inhabited by the [[Warí people]], who referred to the territory as ''Hara Mara''. The name was retained by later colonial administrations and by the independent state.

==Geography==
Haramara consists of approximately 50 islands. The largest island was known during the colonial period as ''Terra de Filips''. Its tropical wet climate, difficult terrain, and dispersed island geography historically made administration and sustained settlement difficult. Colonial authority was concentrated along the coast, while much of the interior remained lightly governed.

==History==
===Warí settlement and Marosian exploration===
The islands were inhabited by Warí communities before the arrival of foreign explorers. Marosian explorers reached Haramara in 1534 and recorded the islands under the name ''Hara Mara''.

===Colonial period===
Zong established a colonial presence in Haramara in 1592, followed by Volisania in 1594. The territory was difficult to govern because of its wet tropical climate, limited transport, and resistance from Warí communities. On Terra de Filips, colonial settlement remained concentrated along portions of the coast.

The Marosian, Zong, and Volisanian authorities agreed to divide the island in the [[Sant Cristofor Agreement of 1623]]. The Volisanian portion was subsequently administered through the [[Viceroyalty of Tapuya]]. Haramara's geographical isolation contributed to relatively peaceful administration, including during periods of political conflict between the colonial powers.

===Marosian Civil War and joint occupation===
Following the collapse of the [[Sultanate of Maros]] during the Marosian Civil War in 1804, the Zong and Volisanian territories jointly occupied the former Marosian colony. After the [[Sanrobonian Revolution]] of 1826–1832, Haramara was permitted to establish its own independent government amid regional concern that Sanrobonia might annex the islands.

===Independence and neutrality===
Haramara remained independent and neutral after independence. During the [[Great Adonian War]], Sanrobonia invaded Haramara as a pre-emptive measure. The occupation ended after Sanrobonian forces withdrew following Haramara's efforts to obtain international support.

===Post-war dictatorship and socialist revolution===
After the war, Sanrobonia helped install a friendly Haramaran leader, [[Antoni Vilar]], who ruled as dictator from 1948 to 1981; a socialist revolution overthrew his son, who had briefly succeeded him, and ended the dictatorship. The new government reorganized the state under the leadership of [[Yakub Ferrer]] and the [[Socialist Party of Haramara]].

Sanrobonia invaded Haramara again in 1981, but the Haramaran government secured international diplomatic and material support and resisted the invasion.

===Civil war and syndicalist government===
The Socialist Party remained in power until 1996, when the president was assassinated by Sanrobonian military services. The assassination triggered a civil war that lasted from 1996 to 1998. The conflict saw the rise of the [[Haramara Syndicalist Army]] (HSA), which received support from [[Balisca]]. The HSA and its allies established a new socialist constitution after the war.

==Politics==
Haramara is a self-described democratic syndicalist state. Political and economic authority is distributed through elected institutions, worker cooperatives, and trade unions. The [[Socialist Party of Haramara]] has remained the principal governing party since the 1996–1998 civil war.

==Economy==
Haramara is heavily dependent on oil exports. Petroleum provides the principal source of export revenue and government income, while worker cooperatives and union-organized enterprises operate in other sectors. The gross domestic product per capita is approximately I$72,234, with an estimated GDP (PPP) of roughly I$587.6 billion.

